  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nCurrently, the short wave is quickly exiting the region to the east \nwith weak ridging building in over the region. Another short wave is \nsituated over the Western US and will be positioned over the Rockies \nlater today. At the surface, high pressure is settling in over the \ncentral portion of the US with the cold front that ushered in much \ncooler air for us now located along the Appalachians. Clear skies \nalong with light and variable winds early this morning is allowing \nample radiational cooling across the forecast area. Many locations \nare seeing their coldest temperatures of the season thus far with \ntemperatures expected to bottom out in the 20s for most. Afternoon \nhighs today will top out about 8 to 10 degrees below normal for this \ntime of year, reaching only into the 40s areawide. \n\nThe approaching short wave will sweep across the region on Tuesday. \nGiven the dry air mass and dominant high pressure in the wake of the \nprevious system, minimal impacts are expected. Cloud cover will \nincrease tonight and very light precipitation will be possible \nacross the western half of the state on Tuesday morning, likely in \nthe form of snow flurries. It is not expected that any wintry precip \nwill impact the forecast area with this quick hitting system. \nHowever, this wave will bring a reinforcing shot of cold air sending \ntemperatures tumbling on Tuesday night. Overnight lows for Tuesday \nand Wednesday nights will dip into the lower 20s for most across the \nstate with highs only reaching into the low to middle 40s. \n\nRidging will begin to build in over the Plains for the end of the \nweek, leading to a slight warmup for the end of the forecast period. \nAfternoon highs for Friday will reach into the middle to upper 50s \nand highs around 60 will be seen for Saturday. Light winds and dry \nconditions are generally expected for the end of the forecast period \nand upcoming holiday weekend.",
